What this solution actually does: it is a scheduled extract-and-replace job that mirrors T24 commission data into five CMS tables. On every timer tick (interval from the external service configuration, 5 s fallback) it records the start of the tick in TOMI, reads the TOMI system date, asks the shared library whether today is a business day (also false on Saturday/Sunday unless forced), reads three parameters (HORA_SALDO_INICIO, HORA_SALDO_FIN, SALDOS_DEBUG_CMP) and compares the machine time with the window. Outside the window or on a non-business day it writes an ERROR log line and an alert row and stops. Inside the window it runs five steps in a fixed order - clientes, esquemas, cobradas, volumen, pendientes. Each step calls a read procedure in the T24 views database that returns the entire result as one XML string, hands that string to a write procedure in the CMS database, which deletes the target rows (the whole table, or the rows whose RECID ends with today's date / this or last month) and re-inserts the XML rows in one transaction, and logs "registros afectados: N". Any exception stops the remaining steps, is logged, and feeds an e-mail throttle that mails two fixed mailboxes after the same message has persisted for 10 minutes. The execution row in TOMI is marked finished only when all five steps succeed. It does not calculate or charge commissions and does not move funds; the correctness of the t24_com_* tables is a dependency of whichever CMS components read them (not identifiable from the portfolio source).
