3. What's Already Good
• Layering and DI: interfaces for both repositories and the service (ICMS, IVT24, IActualizaComisiones), constructor injection in OperacionServicio / ActualizaComisionesService, container Verify() at startup (ServiceHost.cs:99) and a scoped per-tick configuration clone (:90).
• Safe data access: only stored procedures with parameters (CrearParametro); no string-built SQL; XML shredding is a single set-based INSERT ... SELECT per table (e.g. ProcesarComisionesEsquemas.sql:40-79).
• Transactions: each write procedure deletes and inserts inside one transaction, so a table is never left half-replaced by a failed load (the failure is *hidden*, see CMP-FR-14, not corrupting).
• Read procedures follow the right error pattern: all five CATCH ... THROW (e.g. ObtenerComisionesClientes.sql:41-43).
• Cancellation and shutdown: ct.ThrowIfCancellationRequested() (OperacionServicio.cs:63), OperationCanceledException handled (:136), bounded wait/cleanup tokens and finally disposal of the container in TerminacionAsync (ServiceHost.cs:181-193); the base class serializes ticks with a SemaphoreSlim, so a slow refresh cannot overlap itself.
• Operating guard: business day and window are checked before any work, with TimeSpan.TryParse and an explicit alert for a malformed window (OperacionServicio.cs:90-116).
• Date helpers: ConvertirFecha is strict (TryParseExact, invariant culture) and ObtenerPeriodoAnterior handles the January rollover; both are unit-tested including the year boundary (UtilitiesUT.cs).
• Per-step observability: each step logs "registros afectados: N" (ActualizaComisionesService.cs:51, 71, 99, 130, 150), so a normal run is traceable in the file log.
• Naming contract: stored-procedure constants and CREATE PROCEDURE names agree; the SQL project and the application are in one repository and identical to the portfolio SQL copy.
