5. Architecture-Fit Note
This is a scheduled extract-and-replace batch wearing a continuous-timer hosting model: the work is a once-per-business-day style reference-data refresh (five table loads), but the Windows Service wakes every few seconds and repeats it, and its success/failure state is not recorded anywhere durable. Per the portfolio's standing question (section 6 of the control document (./_ORCHESTRATION.md#6-standing-architecture-question-is-the-current-hostingarchitecture-model-right)), the sensible sequence is: fix the real defects first (CMP-FR-14/15/16 in SQL, CMP-FR-1 alerting), then remove duplication and make the tests meaningful (CMP-FR-4, 11, 12), and only then evaluate hosting. A scheduled job (SQL Agent job or an .exe run by a scheduler with the existing business-day and window rules) would match the workload shape better than an always-on service, and a set-based MERGE from a linked-server or staging load would remove the XML-string hop, but both depend on facts not visible here: the required freshness of the t24_com_* tables, who consumes them and when, data volumes, SLAs, hosting constraints, and whether cross-table consistency is required. No re-platforming or rewrite is recommended from this static review.
